WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.700
Remember lesson five's wideband line?

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.200
Before you downsample it, a ready-made
filter, thirty-one numbers long,

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.380
removes every tone
above four thousand hertz.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.620
Today a test engineer
sweeps a tone through it,

5
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.600
from four thousand up
to eight thousand hertz.

6
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.940
She expects silence.

7
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.290
Instead, a ghost glides down to where
voices live, swelling and fading:

8
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.290
nine percent at thirty-five hundred hertz,

9
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.490
five at three thousand,
two at one thousand.

10
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.250
And voices near

11
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.030
thirty-five hundred come out louder.

12
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.310
How can a filter that only multiplies
and adds make anything louder?

13
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.080
Start from the perfect filter,
the ideal low-pass:

14
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.060
below four thousand hertz

15
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.900
everything passes, above it nothing.

16
00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.880
With no feedback,
a filter is just a list of coefficients.

17
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.040
Send in one click,
and the list comes back out.

18
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.660
But the ideal list never ends.

19
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.400
So lesson five kept the middle
thirty-one and threw the rest away.

20
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.360
That's truncation.

21
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.120
At zero hertz the gain is the sum.
Point five.

22
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.600
Add a pair: one point one four.

23
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.840
Another: point nine two.

24
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.640
It overshoots, and swings around one.

25
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.340
Each pair adds an arrow and its mirror,
which leaves a plain number,

26
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.600
sometimes negative.

27
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.540
At forty-five hundred hertz,
every pair pulls down,

28
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.000
to minus point oh nine.

29
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.540
Nine percent of that tone gets out,

30
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.760
upside down,
and lands at thirty-five hundred.

31
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.600
So keep more coefficients? Sixty-three.

32
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:10.960
Two hundred fifty-five.

33
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.080
The bump slides toward
the cliff and narrows,

34
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.560
but it stays nine percent tall.

35
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.640
That's the Gibbs phenomenon.

36
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.920
Why won't it shrink?

37
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.160
Cutting the list
multiplies it by a window:

38
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.500
one for each coefficient you keep,
zero for the rest. A rectangle.

39
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.040
And a window has its own response:

40
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.680
a tall hump,
with side lobes from its sharp edges.

41
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.450
The cut filter's response is the step,
smeared by that hump:

42
00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.130
slide it along,
and add up what's still on the pass side.

43
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.450
When the hump touches the cliff,

44
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.970
the lobes past it are left out,

45
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.650
and they add up to minus nine percent.

46
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.550
Oh, so a hundred and nine is left.

47
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.810
A longer rectangle just narrows the lobes,
so the share stays.

48
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.240
The cure isn't a longer window,

49
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.500
but one without sharp edges: a taper.

50
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.260
The Hamming window fades
from one in the middle

51
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.020
to almost zero at the ends.

52
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.780
Its side lobes almost vanish,
but its hump is twice as wide:

53
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.100
the cliff becomes a slope.

54
00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.550
Your line leaves the top
of its band empty,

55
00:01:58.570 --> 00:01:59.850
so the slope can land there.

56
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.410
Pass up to thirty-four hundred hertz

57
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.870
within one percent,
remove above forty-six hundred

58
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.970
to below one percent.

59
00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.870
The plain cut needs
two hundred seventy-one coefficients.

60
00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.430
Your chip affords forty-seven.

61
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.750
Hamming at forty-five:

62
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.230
point two four percent on both.

63
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.230
The ghost is gone.

64
00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.910
Shape sets the ripple,
length sets the slope.

65
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.640
Next ticket:
you copy the old analog exchange's filter

66
00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.080
with the textbook recipe.

67
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.160
Its cutoff was thirty-four hundred hertz.

68
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.260
The copy's lands
at twenty-three sixty-three.
